  "query_preview": "(This evening through Wednesday)...\nIssued at 145 PM EDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nTonight through Sunday....\n\nMostly quiet weather will be the rule through the weekend as \nsensible weather over central Indiana is dominated by surface high \npressure centered to the N through E, while upper level ridging \nbuilds in from the Plains states. Low dewpoints and longer nights \nwill keep min temps near seasonal normals in the 40s. Clear skies \nwill support strong radiational cooling at night. Despite the \nrecent dry spell, this will lead to late night/early morning \npatchy ground fog in favored low lying areas. Any fog will be \nshort lived as a quick rise of temperatures into upper 60s to mid \n70s occurs for afternoon highs, some 5-10 degrees above normal.\n\nMonday and Tuesday... \n\nThe remnant of the large upper level trough will move out of the \nwestern states into the Plains and MS valley. As this system \nprogress eastward towards Indiana it will deamplify as it encounters \nthe upper ridge centered over the SE states. As this system \napproaches, BL winds will become more southerly. However, a broad \nand dominant area of surface high pressure centered over the SE will \nseverely limit any moisture return. As a result, recent model \nconsensus has backed off on precip chances with this system. The \nnext and much better chance for rain looks to be after the \nforecast period late next week with the arrival of a cold front.",
